>decisions, I think it clearly doesn't do so.

I can't imagine what decisions you're relying on.  I'm relying on
_Sable Communications v. FCC_, 492 U.S. 115 (1989), which struck down
an earlier clause of section 223(b) which banned all "indecent" speech
conducted via telephone for commercial purposes:

"Sexual expression which is indecent but not obscene is protected by
the First Amendment; and the federal parties do not submit that the
sale of such materials to adults could be criminalized solely because
they are indecent. . . .

"In _Butler v. Michigan_, 352 U.S. 380 (1957), a unanimous Court
reversed a conviction under a statute which made it an offense to make
available to the general public materials found to have a potentially
harmful influence on minors.  The Court found the law to be
insufficiently tailored since it denied adults their free speech
rights by allowing them to read only what was acceptable for children.
As Justice Frankfurter said in that case, '[s]urely this is to burn
the house to roast the pig.'  Id. at 383.  In our judgment, this case,
like _Butler_, presents us with 'legislation not reasonably restricted
to the evil with which it is said to deal.' Ibid."

492 U.S. at 126-27.

In short, _Sable_ (and any number of other decisions holding statutes
facially invalid under the First Amendment) holds that while indecency
may be regulated in manner tailored to the government's interest in
protecting children, a flat ban is not an adequately tailored
approach.  Since the Exon bill seeks, among other things, to impose a
ban even broader than that at issue in _Sable_ (where only commercial
speech was at issue), I see no merit in the position that "the context
of past First Amendment decisions" saves the Exon bill.

There are limited circumstances (e.g., _X-Citement Video_, decided
earlier in the current Supreme Court Term) in which a facially
defective speech-infringing statute may be saved by reading additional
requirements into it.  This is not such a case.




